  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 AM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nStill active convection is noted across the eastern third of \nthe FA at this writing. It's waned from its earlier heights wrt\nsvr potential, transitioning moreso to a locally heavy rain \nthreat. It is possible the westward advancing gust front that \njust passed KPAH with a peak wind gust of 31 kts may stir up \nsome convection still to our west, where the higher instability \naxis exists. Otherwise a downward trend is anticipated thru the \nwee morning hours, then a relative wane in activity settles in \nbefore picking up again as we head into the primary/max heating\nhours today. The atmosphere will be juiced similarly to \nyesterday toward the end of the afternoon, so as another blast \nof vort energy tops the ridge with difluent flow aloft and \nheightening CAPE to work with, we'll largely remain under a \nMRGL-SLGT risk for severe. Best shear/energy is removed to the \neast Tuesday, so while there will also be a small chance of \nstorms, they'll be moreso general risk storms to contend with, \nif they can get going Tuesday in the small pop environ. In\nsummary, dirty NW flow energy topping a ridge and diving into \nan extreme heat environ = not good.\n\nSpeaking of that extreme heat/humidity, the swath of 80+F dew \npoints continues to spread/hover over the PAH FA another day \ntoday, and with convective debris cloud cleared early, we'll see\ndangerously high heat indices up to around 115 degrees \nmaterialize region-wide. We've therefore upgraded the portion of\nthe FA still under an Advisory to join the rest of the FA under a\nWarning and will have the entirety of the region thus headlined\ntoday, before the east backs off in a return to its Advisory \nheadline for Tuesday; the west will stay hot/soupy enough to \nsupport another day of up to around 115 degrees, while the east \ntops out up to around 105 degrees Tuesday.\n\nAs the broad/upper anticyclone retrogrades over the back half of\nthe week, we'll see our temperatures and humidity ease off their\nextremes. Highs in the 80s and dew points in the 60s will become\nmore commonplace again, while next rain chances look to hold \noff until next weekend.",
